      "verdict": "partial",
      "gap": "Lauritzen, inside the cited pp. 65-68: (1) p. 65, the claim introducing Theorem 2.44 that, in contrast to the undirected case, the directed Markov properties are equivalent without further regularity assumptions; the handbook says no positivity or intersection is used (body 209-211) but never states the contrast with undirected graphs. (2) p. 68: a Bayesian network defined by structural equations need not have a density with respect to any product measure, but when it has one the results can be strengthened; the page defines the recursive density factorization (F), gives Proposition 2.51 (F holds exactly when the kernels are conditional densities; proof left to the reader as Exercise 2.8), Lemma 2.52 (F gives a factorization over the moral graph and the global Markov property relative to it), the remark that F implies the recursive kernel factorization R, and Theorem 2.53 (under a density with respect to a product measure, F, R, G, L and O are equivalent). Only the first sentence is covered, by the singular chain of thm:p16-measurable-graph-examples; body 5-8 merely says the source treats densities separately as a special case. Property (F), Proposition 2.51, Lemma 2.52 and Theorem 2.53 are not stated or proved anywhere on the route for a product-measure density; the finite counting-measure implication from factorization to the global property (thm:p16-finite-global-markov) covers neither the general variant nor its converse.",
